    "logicalIsomorphs": [
        {
            "case": "pair-anchor-dialect",
            "isomorph": "pair-anchor-common",
            "counts": {
                "case": "Paragraph",
                "isomorph": "Paragraph"
            },
            "binding": {
                "declares": "anchor",
                "sides": ["case"]
            },
            "claim": "An explicit anchor DECLARES a name and binds it to the block it sits on; a link reference definition declares a name and binds it to a target. Same grammar shape -- one block, one name-to-target binding -- and the same subsequent operation at the level of the language: the name becomes addressable, so `[[Note#^id]]` resolves the one and `[id]` resolves the other. Both are read off the grammars. What each implementation then DOES behind that binding -- when it allocates, what it defers, what it skips -- is not part of the pairing; it is the thing being measured, and a pair rewritten until both engines run the same machinery would read 1.00x and measure nothing. DERIVED anchors are deliberately not paired: a heading whose anchor comes from its own text declares nothing, there is no name in the source to bind, and CommonMark has no production that derives one -- so block-heading has no isomorph and is reported as a bound. The two spellings differ in length, so the pair is generated to an equal COUNT of declarations rather than to equal bytes, and each name is indexed so both sides bind the same number of distinct identifiers."
        },
        {
            "case": "pair-specimen-dialect",
            "isomorph": "pair-specimen-common",
            "counts": {
                "case": "Specimen",
                "isomorph": "Footnote"
            },
            "claim": "A specimen definition and a GFM footnote definition are the same production: a one-line opener carrying a label, then block content the opener owns, and a node that leaves ordinary document content for a side list the document holds. Both specs say so in the same words -- Document.specimens, Document.footnotes, not in the content. Each unit also carries a REFERENCE to its label, on both sides, because the two grammars differ on an unreferenced definition: this dialect keeps a specimen nobody cites, and GFM DISCARDS a footnote definition nobody calls. Without the call the reference side parsed to nothing at all and the denominator was cmark-gfm throwing text away -- a bound wearing the name of a comparison. With it, both sides hold one call in ordinary content and one definition owning block content in a side list, which is also the fuller match: declare, and refer."
        },
        {
            "case": "pair-task-dialect",
            "isomorph": "pair-task-common",
            "counts": {
                "case": "ListItem",
                "isomorph": "ListItem"
            },
            "binding": {
                "declares": "marker",
                "sides": ["case", "isomorph"]
            },
            "claim": "A task marker is one Unicode scalar in brackets at the start of a list item's content, removed from the content and stored on the item. GFM's task list item is that production with the scalar restricted to a space or `x`; this dialect keeps the authored scalar instead of mapping it. Same production, one terminal wider -- which is what makes this pair, and what it measures. Both sides build ListItem, so an equal count proves nothing on its own; the pair names `marker` and the audit requires every item on BOTH sides to carry one, because an item whose marker went unread is still a list item. The reference is cmark-gfm."
        },
        {
            "case": "pair-span-dialect",
            "isomorph": "pair-span-common",
            "counts": {
                "case": "Span",
                "isomorph": "Link"
            },
            "claim": "A bracketed span and an inline link are the same production: an opening bracket, a body parsed as inline content, a closing bracket, and an immediately following paired-delimiter suffix taken raw and decoded into fields of the one node the production builds. The dialect spec says so directly -- a span is decided in the same bracket alternation as a link, and a valid link wins the position -- so the two are alternatives at one point in the grammar and not merely alike. The suffix is where they differ and nothing else is: `{.c}` decodes into attributes, `(/c)` decodes into a destination. Neither looks anything up. The two spellings are the same length here, so the pair is byte for byte as well as count for count."
        },
        {
            "case": "pair-xlink-dialect",
            "isomorph": "pair-xlink-common",
            "counts": {
                "case": "CrossLink",
                "isomorph": "Link"
            },
            "claim": "A cross link and an empty-text inline link are the same production: fixed bracket delimiters around a body that is NOT parsed as inline, producing one leaf node that stores the body as a destination and has no children. Neither resolves it -- the cross-link spec is explicit that the parser stores the strings and the application finds the resource, and an inline link's destination is likewise stored, not looked up -- so the subsequent operation matches too. The empty text is deliberate: a cross link has no inline body, and pairing it against a link that did have one would hand the reference an inline parse this side never performs. Not the same length, so the pair is generated to an equal COUNT."
        },
        {
            "case": "pair-cite-dialect",
            "isomorph": "pair-cite-common",
            "counts": {
                "case": "Cite",
                "isomorph": "Link"
            },
